WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.810
Film Courage: What’s your process for developing
characters?

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:03.810
How do you go about it?

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:08.700
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Producer: Well the
character kind of develops itself.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:14.089
Basically what you do when you are dealing
with that is kind of like a checklist rather

00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:18.740
than you’re developing the character, you
are letting the character develop himself

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:25.330
but you say…she has to have the following
things.

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.560
What’s her problem for example?

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.939
Is that clear?

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:35.950
Because if that is not clear your story is
not going to hook the audience.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:42.260
What is her problem interfering with?

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:48.630
I call this mission in life like usually it’s
her motivation in the story (her problem)

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.789
is interfering with her mission in life.

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:57.739
She is somebody who wants to become a nurse
but something violent happens in the opening

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:04.220
scene and it seems to make her if you want
to save this person you have to do something

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:12.030
violent back and therefore her motivation
if she is going to save this person interferes

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:20.520
with her mission and those are kind of two
parts of the checklist and you go on to to

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:26.040
talk about how does she change (this is called
her arc).

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.360
What is the arc of her change?

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.000
How is she different at the end of the story
because a lot of times we are covering stories

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.710
that are submitted to us.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.310
Well you know the characters don’t change.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:40.570
She’s exactly the way she was at the end
that she is at the beginning.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.610
So why?

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:44.750
We don’t get off on that.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:48.600
We are not satisfied by a story where the
characters don’t change.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:55.409
So these are kind of checklists that you apply
to characters once you get them going starting

00:01:55.409 --> 00:02:04.560
with that intriguing situation and then adding
along the way by just checking them against

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.690
traditional characters that work well.

